            "<p>Taratarini Temple</p>",
            "<p>Ganjam (Berhampur)</p>",
            "<p>Important Shakti pitha; twin Goddess</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Biraja Temple</p>",
            "<p>Jajpur</p>",
            "<p>Important Shakti pitha</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Maa Mangala Temple</p>",
            "<p>Kakatpur, Puri</p>",
            "<p>Dream deity of chariot artists (Daitapatis)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kapilash Temple</p>",
            "<p>Dhenkanal</p>",
            "<p>Chandrashekhara form of Shiva; hill top</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u0lz9bu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "11.2 Kalinga Architecture Styles"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "igbd8fe",
      "content": "Odishan (Kalinga) temple architecture has three distinct types:"
    },
    {
      "id": "ivc360s",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Style</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description &amp; Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Rekha Deula</p>",
            "<p>Curvilinear tower (shikhara); Sanctum sanctorum; e.g., Lingaraj, Jagannath</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Pidha Deula</p>",
            "<p>Pyramidal roof; assembly hall (jagamohan); e.g., Konark Jagamohan</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Khakhara Deula</p>",
            "<p>Oblong curved roof; Shakti temples; e.g., Vaital Deula</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "f7l3axy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "11.3 UNESCO Heritage Sites in Odisha"
    },
    {
      "id": "6y5nu05",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Konark Sun Temple (1984) – UNESCO World Heritage Site",
        "Simlipal Biosphere Reserve – UNESCO Biosphere Reserve Network",
        "Chilika Lake – Ramsar Wetland (1981); also under UNESCO monitoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "o6hnm2v",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "11.4 Classical Dance – Odissi"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ln2jsja",
      "content": "Odissi is one of the eight classical dance forms of India. It originated in the temples of Odisha (especially Jagannath Temple, Puri). The dancers were called 'Maharis' (temple devadasis). Guru Pankaj Charan Das, Guru Kelucharan Mohapatra, and Guru Deba Prasad Das revived Odissi in the 20th century."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Classical Dance (Odissi)",
        "Folk Dances of Odisha"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Origin & Context",
          "Rooted in ancient temple rituals (Mahari tradition) and classical treatises like Natya Shastra",
          "Evolved from community festivals, martial traditions, and seasonal celebrations"
        ],
        [
          "Structure & Grammar",
          "Strict adherence to codified mudras, postures (Tribhangi, Chauka), and classical music",
          "Flexible, rhythmic movements focused on group harmony and folk musical instruments"
        ],
        [
          "Prominent Examples",
          "Odissi",
          "Chhau, Gotipua, Dalkhai, Ghumura, Sambalpuri"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "2j2ywpx"
    },
